.TH btctax-income-project 1  btctax "btctax manual"
.SH NAME
project \- Project this year\*(Aqs return onto the household description the two independent tax engines take, and print it (JSON). Run it before you export
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBproject\fR <\fB\-\-year\fR> [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Project this year\*(Aqs return onto the household description the two independent tax engines take, and print it (JSON). Run it before you export.
.PP
btctax\*(Aqs figures are validated against two engines that share no lineage — OpenTaxSolver and the Policy Simulation Library\*(Aqs Tax\-Calculator — but both are fed a household description, not a btctax return, so until now only the built\-in corpus could be checked. This prints your return as that description: wages, interest, dividends, the Schedule D gains, Schedule C net profit, unemployment, the four Schedule A components, which deduction line 12 claimed, the HSA deduction, and your dependents with their ages, the credit box each one lands in, blindness and your own age.
.PP
WHAT IS CHECKED IS THE DESCRIPTION, NOT THE PACKET. Everything the engines are compared against — including btctax\*(Aqs own column — is computed from the description below, so a figure the description cannot carry is missing from every column and produces silence rather than a disagreement. Read `not_carried` before you read the verdicts.
.PP
It carries NO identity — not because it strips one, but because the description has no name, Social Security number, address, employer or payer field to put one in. It is safe to paste.
.PP
It also prints `not_carried`: every figure on your return that the engines\*(Aq description cannot express, with the reason. That list is the honest part — a filer with medical expenses, student\-loan interest or a prior\-year capital\-loss carryforward is described to the engines WITHOUT them, so a disagreement on those lines is the description\*(Aqs, not btctax\*(Aqs.
.PP
And it prints `refused` when btctax will not COMPUTE this return — the same screens `btctax report` runs, on your own inputs. The row still prints so you can see what the engines would be told, but `check_return.py` exits 2 on it: a return that does not file has nothing to reconcile.
.PP
`scripts/oracle/check_return.py` consumes this and runs both engines on it. Nothing is written and nothing leaves your machine.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-year\fR \fI<YEAR>\fR
The tax year (e.g. 2024)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
